      <h2>Pitco vs Frymaster, head-to-head for LA buyers</h2>
      <p>
        Pitco (Middleby family — via Blodgett 1981 acquisition chain) and Frymaster (Ali Group family — via the
        July 2022 Welbilt acquisition) are cross-corporate competitors. Both are premium US commercial fryer
        lines with integrated auto-filtration as their flagship feature. Different parts pipelines, different
        sibling-brand single-call paths, different LA segment fit. Here's how they compare in LA service
        experience:
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Oil filtration mechanism.</b> Pitco Solstice Supreme uses FilterMate, a pump-and-paper auto-filter with
        O-ring sealed manifold. Frymaster FilterQuick (FQE electric, FQG gas) uses a different manifold-based
        auto-filter architecture. Failure modes differ; parts supply differs (Middleby distribution vs Ali Group
        NA distribution); service protocols differ. Both systems work well when maintained; both degrade when
        maintenance is skipped.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Market position.</b> Pitco has one of the broadest US install bases across independent operations and
        non-McDonald's chains, with the longest-running auto-filtration brand history (Frialator 1959 invention).
        Frymaster has the McDonald's OEM relationship spanning 50+ years (H17 legacy and current FilterQuick
        generations), which makes Frymaster ubiquitous across McDonald's franchise operations and adjacent QSR
        ecosystems.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>Build and controls.</b> Roughly comparable premium-tier engineering. Pitco leans on Bow NH (since 1918)
        plus Concord NH (2021 expansion) manufacturing heritage; Frymaster leans on Shreveport, Louisiana
        manufacturing and deep McDonald's franchise integration. Control interfaces differ but functionality is
        similar.
      </p>
      <p>
        <b>LA buyer recommendation.</b> For non-franchise independent operations: Pitco Solstice Supreme is usually
